      "id": 1,
      "text": "Joseph formally named itself in 1880 after Chief Joseph of the Nez Perce people.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph%2C_Oregon"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Surrounded by the Wallowa mountain range, Joseph is often referred to as the Little Switzerland of America.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://josephoregon.com/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The economy of Joseph originally relied on land, grain, and stock before a lumber mill opened in 1908.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph%2C_Oregon"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Bronze foundries opened in the local area in 1982 after the timber industry collapsed during the 1980s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph%2C_Oregon"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Main Street in Joseph features a bronze Art Walk that explores the area's history, wildlife, and culture.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://josephoregon.com/"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Alpenfest in Joseph resembles an Oktoberfest but features more yodeling and alphorn playing.",
